statement:
A physicist has a finite family of particles called imons. Some pairs of imons are entangled. He may perform the following operations, one at a time:

\begin{enumerate}
\item If an imon is entangled with an odd number of other imons, he may destroy it.
\item He may double the whole family: for every imon $I$ he creates a copy $I'$. Two copies $I',J'$ are entangled exactly when $I,J$ were entangled, and each copy $I'$ is entangled with its original $I$. No other entanglements are added or removed.
\end{enumerate}

Prove that the physicist can perform a sequence of operations after which no two remaining imons are entangled.
